Z||000000||AL||02-07-2001||09:17 PM||||<BR>where is the fuel pump relay? isn't it the one on the left side in the deck?the reason i ask is,when i turn my indy over i can't here the pump turning on, but when i let it crank a couple of times then i know the other relay is turning it on,so which one is it or what other thing can it be.the fuel pump is new.<B>AL</B> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/AL_sig.gif"> <P>------------------<BR> ||||reg||1
Z||000001||Alex4mula||02-07-2001||09:46 PM||||I think is the right one. You can interchange the two of them to check if it is the relay what is bad.||||reg||1
Z||000002||Oreif||02-07-2001||10:04 PM||||There is also a Fuel Pump relay as part of the oil pressure sensor too. No pressure no fuel.||||reg||1
Z||000003||AL||02-07-2001||10:16 PM||||so which one is it? i did change both out and still the same thing.||||reg||1
Z||000004||Oreif||02-07-2001||11:46 PM||||Silly question, What about the fuse under the dash? A bad relay could pop the fuse. I don't think the Fuel Pump has a fuse-link.||||reg||1
Z||000005||AL||02-08-2001||01:27 AM||||it's not the fuse either.||||reg||1
Z||000006||MontanaFiero||02-08-2001||03:27 AM||||Al, I ended up replacing the pump. Same symptoms. It's not as hard as it appears. I used 4 jackstands to raise the car and two floor jacks to lower the gas tank. I did this curb-side and with a FULL tank of gas. Just make sure you use a BRASS punch to remove the pump assembly so you don't spark.  Took one day- after spending three days swapping computers, relays, and tracing wires.<P>BTW, don't try to plug the pump harness directly into the battery to test it. One of the wires is for the fuel level, and the color codes in Haynes and Chilton are WRONG. <P>The pump is controlled by the right relay and also by the oil pressure switch. The oil pressure has to reach 7 psi(?) before it will turn the pump on (backup in case of a relay failure). Both of these have to go through the computer first, so the relays are not hardwired to the pump. Also check ECM fuse.<P>Hope this helps.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><BR>||||reg||1
Z||000007||danny boy||02-08-2001||07:08 PM||||Well, thanks everybody. You just helped me to fix my mom's truck.(2.8 S10.) ||||reg||1
Z||000008||theogre||02-08-2001||07:45 PM||||For reference.... the FP relay is controlled by the ECM. The Relay and oil pressure switch contacts are wired in parrallel. Closing either contacts turns the pump on. The fuel circuit does not "go thru" the ECM. ||||reg||1
Z||000009||AL||02-08-2001||10:42 PM||||ok first off i replaced the fuel pump,when i started it i could hear the pump comming on,now when my ecm took a dump i changed it out and then i couldn't here the fuel pump comming on,next i changed relays and still nothing i checked fuses and it was ok, so what else is there to check i did everything and still i can't start it up right away.i really don't want to waiit for the oil relay to turn it on.   ||||reg||1
Z||000010||MontanaFiero||02-09-2001||03:17 AM||||Try cranking the motor long enough to build up significant oil pressure. This should engage the fuel pump (through the oil pressure switch) and the engine should start.<P>If this works, you still may have an ECM issue, because the ECM controls the relay. (thanks Theogre, I stand corrected)  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/redface.gif"><P>If not, then you are either:<BR><UL TYPE=SQUARE><P><LI>  Not getting power to the relay (fuel pump fuse)<P><LI>  New fuel pump is a piece of s***<BR></UL><P>Did you clean your tank before you put your new pump to work? It is very common to have new pumps fail immediately for the same reason as the old one... DIRT.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif"><P>Good Luck.<BR><P>------------------<BR>&gt;&gt; '87 Coupe 4 cyl, auto &lt;&lt;<BR>&gt;&gt; '86 SE V6, 4 speed- project &lt;&lt;||||reg||1
Z||000011||CHARLIE FERGUSON||02-09-2001||03:00 PM||||the only problem i've had with my car was after i drove it awhile and turned it off it would not start for 30 min to an hour later. after i replace the distributor and coil the problem still existed, after talking to a friend of mine he said to check the fuel pump relay sure enough thats what it was. the fuel pump relay is on the right you can test by switching with the left one then turn the key and listen for the fuel pump to come on. by the way a new relay cost me about 8 bucks at autozone.||||reg||1
Z||000012||lou_dias||02-09-2001||03:25 PM||||You replaced the distributor...did it come with a new ignition module?  A faulty one will over heat and work for a short while until it overheats again.||||reg||1
Z||000013||theogre||02-09-2001||08:19 PM||||Al,<P>If the fuel pump spun with the old ECM and not with the "new" one then I'd have to say the new one is hosed.<P>If there is a terminal in location G on the aldl then you can connect that terminal to 12V and see if the pump will spin up. That terminal is a direct feed to the pump. If terminal G is blank then the test connector is in the engine bay someplace. (On the latter cars it should be terminal G but I think that the older ones are in back someplace.)<P>look in the ECM section of my cave if you can't positvily ID the terminal G. If you dump 12v in the wrong terminal you'll fry the ECM. <A HREF="http://fierocave.shorturl.com" TARGET=_blank>The Ogre's Fiero Cave</A>||||reg||1
Z||000014||AL||02-09-2001||09:57 PM||||the car works but i want to hear the fuel pump come on when i turn the key,i will try to feed power dirrectly to the pump.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000015||MontanaFiero||02-09-2001||11:11 PM||||Sounds like the ECM to me, (you did try swaping relays, right?) <P>-the car could be starting just because of the oil pressure switch-- if you can't hear the pump with NO oil pressure (i.e. turning the key to "on", then It's got to be the relay or the ECM-<P>&gt;&gt; Hey, how much for the EGR tube?&lt;&lt;<P><P>------------------<BR>&gt;&gt; '87 Coupe 4 cyl, auto &lt;&lt;<BR>&gt;&gt; '86 SE V6, 4 speed- project &lt;&lt;||||reg||1
Z||000016||AL||02-11-2001||06:09 PM||||it seems that the fuel pump comes on but it doesn't seem to push the gas at all,i'm thinking maybe the line got blocked off,gonna have to lower the tank again.||||reg||1
Z||000017||Jake_Dragon||02-12-2001||12:15 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by AL:<BR><B>it seems that the fuel pump comes on but it doesn't seem to push the gas at all,i'm thinking maybe the line got blocked off,gonna have to lower the tank again.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Check for hose leaks in the tank.<P>Put in a new pump, and found the hose from the pump to the tube had a slit in it so I had pressure to a open container but as soon as I close the system the slit would open up and just cycle fuel in the tank. The new pump has clamps where the old one didnt so I know it is tight.<BR>Good luck.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||3
Z||000018||AL||02-12-2001||07:27 PM||||this car is giving me a battle! it seems that when i took the old ecm out thinking that it was fried i put in a new one.well it worked fine but then i couldn't hear the fuel pump comming on with the key on and the car worked fine,now the car doesn't get gas at all to the throtle body but i can hear the fuel pump coming on but no go,so then i decided to put in the original ecm and now that one is giving me a code 12 when before it didn't,so now i have to see why this is happening,i guess it's true that the 84's were troublesome,o well this indy is not gonna beat me. ||||reg||1
